From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 15:38:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: let another CPU dispatch Darwin host events when CPU0 is
 late

Darwin has no per-thread SIGIO, so CPU0 alone collects the host's
descriptor events: at its clock tick, when it leaves idle, and when the
notification thread signals it while it idles. A guest whose CPU0 is
busy sees an event only at the next tick, and the host can keep that
carrier from running for hundreds of milliseconds under load, so a
descriptor that is ready waits for as long as the host holds CPU0 while
other CPUs run.

Let any CPU dispatch, one at a time. triggered[] and the IRQ core's
per-IRQ active and pending flags belong to whoever dispatches, so a
token guards them: a compare-and-swap that never waits, taken around
every call of sigio_handler(). CPU0 tries at every tick and idle exit,
as before. Any other CPU tries only when no CPU has begun a dispatch for
two ticks (20 ms), at its own tick and at its idle exit. A CPU that
finds the token taken skips, and the edge stays in the dispatch queue
for the holder's loop or the next dispatch; each EV_CLEAR edge is
returned by exactly one kevent(), and the token orders the dispatchers.
Generic irq.c is unchanged.

An edge can arrive after the holder's last kevent() and before it
releases the token, and CPU0 can try in that window. CPU0 therefore
records a miss when its compare-and-swap fails and looks at the owner
again, taking the token if it is free; the holder looks at the miss
after its release store and dispatches once more if it is set. The two
sides store and then load in sequentially consistent order, so one of
them always sees the other, and no timed poll is left in CPU0's idle
entry.

The notification thread never waits for CPU0 or for anything CPU0 holds,
so it reads the next edge while CPU0 is held, even in the middle of the
idle entry. It and CPU0's idle entry share two flags in sequentially
consistent order, each side storing one and loading the other: the
thread stores that an edge is pending and signals CPU0 if CPU0 is in
its wait; CPU0 stores that it is in the wait and, if an edge is pending,
does not sleep. SIGUSR2 stays blocked on CPU0's carrier outside the
idle pselect(), so a signal that arrives late ends the next wait early
at the worst. For an edge that no dispatch has begun after within
20 ms, whether CPU0 is busy or idle and not running, the thread writes a
byte to the wake pipe of a secondary that is idle, which dispatches from
its idle exit; no signal is sent, so nothing interrupts a CPU in guest
code. It asks again every 20 ms until a dispatch begins, sooner while no
secondary is idle.

While CPU0 is not in its wait, the thread does not read every edge:
after one it parks on a second kqueue, which holds the stop event and a
user event that CPU0's next idle entry triggers, and waits there for as
long as the 20 ms allows. The edges that arrive meanwhile coalesce
unread, so a busy guest costs the thread a wake-up per interval and not
one per edge. Parking is a third flag in the same store-then-load
order, so a thread that parks is woken by CPU0's next entry, and an edge
for a CPU0 that idles is never left to the 20 ms. An edge read after a
park counts its 20 ms from the later of the previous read and the start
of the last dispatch, and only a dispatch that begins after the read
serves it.

Secondaries can take the token from the platform's prepare step of a
system suspend until they go offline, and throughout s2idle, where they
stay online. A dispatch does nothing then, because the IRQs are
suspended, so this is no worse than before. A healthy CPU0 dispatches
within a tick, so nothing is taken over.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
